<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-normal-line-pts-R2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Point-normal form for a line in <m> \R^2 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case:
	<ol>

		<li>
			Determine a <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-normal-vector" text="custom">normal vector</xref></term>
			for the line that passes through the points <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Sketch the line in the <m> xy </m>-plane,
			and draw your normal vector with its initial point at one of either <m> P </m> or <m> Q </m>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Write the equation of the line in <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-point-normal-form" text="custom">point-normal form</xref></term>,
			then expand and re-write in typical algebraic form.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> P(2,1), Q(3,3) </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			The directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m> is parallel to the line through <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m>.
			Calculate the associated vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> as
			<md> \uvec{v} = (3 - 2, 3 - 1) = (1, 2) </md>.
			Using the pattern from <xref ref="activity-orthog-guess-orthog-in-plane" />
			(see also <xref ref="subsubsection-orthog-concepts-orthog-in-R2" />),
			we can create normal vector <m> \uvec{n} = (2, -1) </m>.
		</p></li>

		<li>
			<image width="43.2%">
				<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-normal-line-pts-R2-quad1">
					<xi:include href="normal-line-pts-R2.d/quad1.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
				<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a normal vector to a line in the plane. </shortdescription>
				<description><p>
					A diagram consisting of a vector between two points,
					the line through those two points,
					and a vector normal to the line.
					The points <m> P(2,1) </m> and <m> Q(3,3) </m> are plotted in the <m> xy </m>-plane,
					and the directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m> is drawn between them and labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m>.
					The line that passes through <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is also drawn and labelled as <m> \ell </m>.
					Finally,
					the vector <m> \uvec{n} = (2,-1) </m> is drawn with initial point at <m> P </m> and terminal point <m> (4,0) </m>,
					meeting <m> \ell </m> at a right angle at <m> P </m>.
				</p></description>
			</image>
		</li>

		<li><p>
			Point-normal form (using point <m>P</m>):
			<md> \dotprod{ (2,-1) }{\bbrac{ (x,y) - (2,1) }} = 0 </md>.
			Expanded algebraic form:
			<md> 2 x - y = 3 </md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> P(-2,2), Q(2,-1) </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			The directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m> is parallel to the line through <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m>.
			Calculate the associated vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> as
			<md> \uvec{v} = \bbrac{2 - (-2), -1 - 2} = (4, -3) </md>.
			Using the pattern from <xref ref="activity-orthog-guess-orthog-in-plane" />
			(see also <xref ref="subsubsection-orthog-concepts-orthog-in-R2" />),
			we can create normal vector <m> (-3, -4) </m>,
			though maybe we would prefer to take the negative:
			<m> \uvec{n} = (3,4) </m>.
		</p></li>

		<li>
			<image width="76.4%">
				<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-normal-line-pts-R2-quad2-to-quad4">
					<xi:include href="normal-line-pts-R2.d/quad2-to-quad4.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
				<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a normal vector to a line in the plane. </shortdescription>
				<description><p>
					A diagram consisting of a vector between two points,
					the line through those two points,
					and a vector normal to the line.
					The points <m> P(-2,2) </m> and <m> Q(2,-1) </m> are plotted in the <m> xy </m>-plane,
					and the directed line segment <m> \abray{PQ} </m> is drawn between them and labelled as vector <m> \uvec{v} </m>.
					The line that passes through <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is also drawn and labelled as <m> \ell </m>.
					Finally,
					the vector <m> \uvec{n} = (3,4) </m> is drawn with initial point at <m> Q </m> and terminal point <m> (5,3) </m>,
					meeting <m> \ell </m> at a right angle at <m> Q </m>.
				</p></description>
			</image>
		</li>

		<li><p>
			Point-normal form (using point <m>P</m>):
			<md> \dotprod{ (3,4) }{\bbrac{ (x,y) - (-2,2) }} = 0 </md>.
			Expanded algebraic form:
			<md> 3 x + 4 y = 2 </md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
